WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:05.680
When George Lucas came up with the idea of
Star Wars in the mid-1970s, setting an adventure

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.910
of this scope in outer space had never been
done before.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.480
So how did he get the film to look so good
when there were no visual effects companies

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.070
up to the challenge at the time?

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.750
He started his own.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.100
Industrial Light and Magic was formed to handle
the special effects in Star Wars, and they're

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.220
still revolutionizing the industry with their
work.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.990
Let's peek behind the scenes at what Star
Wars movies look like before they work their

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.990
magic.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.300
A New Hope

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.340
The original trilogy is known for its use
of practical effects, but in truth those films

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.950
relied on visual trickery just as much as
their successors.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:44.060
Sprawling GCI backgrounds were not an option
when the first Star Wars movie was being made,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.300
so the effects artists at ILM came up with
some ingenious alternatives.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.620
Matte glass paintings were painstakingly created
by hand and then added to scenes later, but

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.140
a different technique was needed to bring
the movie's many spaceships to life.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.270
Former ILM man John Dykstra told Den of Geek:

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:06.370
"Back in the days of Star Wars, we kind of
walked into an empty warehouse and sat on

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.420
the floor and went, 'How are we going to do
this?

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.400
The producers, Gary Kurtz of Fox and George
Lucas, took an incredible risk by listening

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.000
to what myself and my collaborators had to
say with regard to how to do this, because

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.170
we were inventing this stuff from scratch."

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.970
When it came to Han Solo's Millennium Falcon,
Dykstra decided to create a model and film

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:30.550
it in front of a blue screen using an early
computer-controlled camera, which allowed

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:34.730
his team to add all of the outer space details
in post.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.520
The Empire Strikes Back

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.340
Dykstra and his team won an Oscar for their
revolutionary work on Star Wars, but Lucas

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.140
chose not to bring Dykstra along when he moved
his base of operations to San Francisco.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.160
It was here that ILM got to work on the next
movie, but despite the success of the first

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:55.360
film, budget restraints were still a problem
when it came to making The Empire Strikes

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.360
Back.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.420
As VFX supervisor Richard Edlund later pointed
out:

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.299
"Computer graphics were extremely expensive,
very slow and the resolution was always a

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:05.540
problem."

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.660
Luckily for Edlund and Lucas, the team they'd
assembled proved more than capable of delivering

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.500
results within their means.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.510
The film's cinematographer Mark Virgo compared
this era in cinematic history to the lunar

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:23.790
program, referring to the period as an amazing
cooperative of artists, imagination, optics,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.970
engineering, and home-brewed software.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:27.970
Return of the Jedi

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:33.550
By the time 1983's Return of the Jedi went
into production, the ILM crew had found their

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:34.550
groove.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:38.460
Master of puppets Phil Tippett was brought
in to head up the creature shop that ultimately

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:43.690
created the Ewoks, and the rest of the effects
work was divided between Richard Edlund, Ken

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.099
Ralston, and Dennis Muren.

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:50.800
Among the jobs that fell to Muren was the
speeder bike chase, one of the hardest sequences

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.820
in the film with 100 total effects shots.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.080
Muren explained:

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.099
"Because the bikes were going to be blue screen,
the backgrounds didn't require that many different

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:04.980
shots to do, because we could use them over
and over again [...] I got the idea to do

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:05.980
it with a Steadicam.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.070
I called Garrett Brown up, who invented it,
and he flew out from back east and we did

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:14.980
a test out in Samuel Taylor Park out here,
with him just walking along with the camera,

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.040
shooting one frame a second.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.459
And the thing worked."

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:22.950
Muller and Brown spent several days out in
the woods, just filming trees from different

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.020
angles, then matched the footage up when they
went back to the studio to film the actors

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.319
in front of blue screens.

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:30.980
The Phantom Menace

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.980
George Lucas' long-awaited return to the Star
Wars universe didn't fail financially, but

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:42.320
it let down a number of fans, who were quick
to turn on new CGI character Jar Jar Binks.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.640
Despite the overwhelmingly negative reaction
to the character, Ahmed Best - the man who

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.819
played the Gungan - is proud of the fact that
he broke new ground with Jar Jar.

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:51.990
He explained:

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:56.520
"One of the biggest reasons I took it was
because of the challenge of it, it hadn't

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.840
been done before.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.730
There was no Andy Serkis and Gollum, no Navi
from Avatar.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:06.910
[…] I was working with George to pioneer
this new character form of acting and storytelling."

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:12.459
Unlike Best, the actor who played Darth Maul
only has fond memories of The Phantom Menace,

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:15.050
his first experience on a movie set.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:20.000
Martial artist and actor Ray Park was initially
only brought in to film some test footage,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.550
but Lucas liked his work so much that he decided
to cast him as the Sith Lord.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:29.409
Park's primary fighting style is wushu, but
he also practiced gymnastics and ballet in

00:04:29.409 --> 00:04:34.090
preparation for his big fight scene with Obi-Wan
Kenobi and Qui-Gon Jinn.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.979
Once the choreography was worked out, the
scene was filmed in front a custom-built blue

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:43.650
set and handed off to ILM, just one of the
many sequences they were required to complete.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.629
Attack of the Clones

00:04:45.629 --> 00:04:51.210
The ongoing advances in CGI meant that Lucas
could set stories in parts of his galaxy that

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.389
were previously inaccessible, which he took
full advantage of in Attack of the Clones.

00:04:55.389 --> 00:04:56.569
He explained:

00:04:56.569 --> 00:05:00.469
"That was the fun part of writing the project,
I wasn't limited.

00:05:00.469 --> 00:05:04.710
Whatever my imagination could come up with
I would just put down on the page and say,

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.610
'We'll worry about this later.'"

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:11.129
Animation director Rob Coleman was initially
under the impression that some of the cityscapes

00:05:11.129 --> 00:05:15.789
and backgrounds Lucas required for Episode
II were going to have some practical elements,

00:05:15.789 --> 00:05:17.990
but he soon learned this wasn't the case.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:24.309
Of the 2000 effects shots in Attack of the
Clones, around half include completely computer-generated

00:05:24.309 --> 00:05:25.309
characters.

00:05:25.309 --> 00:05:30.280
A team of 60 animators worked alongside 340
ILM artists and technicians to render some

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.960
of the well-known Star Wars faces in CGI,
including a certain little green Jedi master.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.129
Rob Coleman explained:

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:41.449
"The biggest technical challenge for me was
animating Yoda.

00:05:41.449 --> 00:05:45.990
That was due to Frank Oz having created that
character in The Empire Strikes Back as a

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:46.990
puppet.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.289
This time, Yoda [...] is much more active
and prominent.

00:05:49.289 --> 00:05:52.469
He actually runs and has a lightsaber battle."

00:05:52.469 --> 00:05:57.199
Yoda's duel with Count Dooku was one of the
film's big visual effects set pieces, but

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:01.559
it required some human input from Christopher
Lee, who was one of the many cast members

00:06:01.559 --> 00:06:05.259
forced to work with blue screens for long
periods of time.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:07.210
Revenge of the Sith

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:12.289
According to Ian McDiarmid, who played Sheev
Palpatine, the use of green screen was really

00:06:12.289 --> 00:06:16.719
ramped up for the third and final film in
Lucas' prequel trilogy, which had the most

00:06:16.719 --> 00:06:18.370
effects shots yet.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:19.370
He told The Telegraph:

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:22.899
"So much of the filming took place in front
of a green screen.

00:06:22.899 --> 00:06:27.349
I used to feel great sympathy for Ewan and
Hayden because they had so much fighting to

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:30.569
do and quite often had to fight imagined enemies.

00:06:30.569 --> 00:06:34.909
[...] George often hadn't decided on details
of the film, so Ewan and Hayden didn't know

00:06:34.909 --> 00:06:38.899
whether they were filming an ironic moment
or whether they were about to die."

00:06:38.899 --> 00:06:44.259
Episode III also introduced us to General
Grievous, the lightsaber-wielding cyborg who

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:48.369
held the title of Supreme Commander of the
Droid Army during the Clone Wars.

00:06:48.369 --> 00:06:53.199
Upon receiving the design for the character,
animation director Rob Coleman knew ILM were

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:56.249
going to have a tough job breathing life into
this one.

00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:57.249
He explained:

00:06:57.249 --> 00:07:02.639
"Since his movement was so restricted, we
decided to have him talk with his hands, because,

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:06.849
although there is a grill pattern where his
mouth would be, there are no articulating

00:07:06.849 --> 00:07:08.280
bits for his mouth.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.300
No lip-sync or facial expressions.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:11.550
No brow to move.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.189
No indication of what he is feeling or thinking."

00:07:14.189 --> 00:07:17.599
Grievous wasn't the only villain without eyebrows
in this movie.

00:07:17.599 --> 00:07:22.710
It's in Episode III that we get to see Anakin
Skywalker become Darth Vader after he's badly

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:26.879
burned in a battle with Obi-Wan, a scene which
had its own effects challenges.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:31.669
Hayden Christensen wasn't originally going
to be the person in the iconic suit, but in

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:34.550
the end he convinced Lucas to let him play
the part.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:38.270
"I begged and pleaded and they were nice enough
to build a Darth Vader suit that actually

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:39.409
fit me."

00:07:39.409 --> 00:07:41.129
The Force Awakens

00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:42.270
After working with J.J.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:47.409
Abrams on his Star Trek reboot, visual effects
supervisor Roger Guyett rejoined the director

00:07:47.409 --> 00:07:52.459
going into The Force Awakens, and the pair's
partnership proved as fruitful as ever.

00:07:52.459 --> 00:07:58.379
Episode Seven made over two billion dollars
globally, while Guyett and his team were nominated

00:07:58.379 --> 00:08:03.669
for an Academy Award for their work, much
of which incorporated practical effects.

00:08:03.669 --> 00:08:07.270
In particular, real models were often used
for the little BB-8 droid.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:09.209
Guyett told Art of VFX.

00:08:09.209 --> 00:08:13.540
"From the beginning we knew we wanted BB-8
to be as practical as possible, so that the

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.919
actors could interact with him, and of course
J.J.

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:17.610
could direct him on set."

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:22.439
Removing the puppeteers from shots later on
proved tricky, but Guyett had nothing but

00:08:22.439 --> 00:08:24.029
praise for them.

00:08:24.029 --> 00:08:28.169
Despite all the effort that went into making
BB-8 real, there were still a number of scenes

00:08:28.169 --> 00:08:32.680
in which the character had to be rendered
digitally out of necessity—around a third

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:33.990
of all shots, in fact.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:38.200
"Obviously some of the characters just couldn't
be done physically, and those occasions, J.J.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:42.470
was more than happy to push the digital technology
of fully digital actors."

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:47.950
For all the practical work, Guyett estimates
that The Force Awakens contains around 2,100

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:49.720
effects shots.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:52.320
Rogue One: A Star Wars Story

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.290
The first Star Wars spinoff went in a new
direction in terms of both narrative and style,

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.740
and most critics were impressed with the result.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:04.250
One of the major talking points was the digital
resurrection of Peter Cushing as Grand Moff

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:08.970
Tarkin, whose character was portrayed by Guy
Henry via motion capture.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:13.440
VFX supervisor John Knoll was able to track
down a cast of Cushing's face made for the

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:18.600
1984 movie Top Secret, which gave them a huge
head start when it came to recreating the

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:20.120
late actor's likeness.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:25.100
The process took a whopping 18 months to complete
and was done with the permission of Cushing's

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:26.100
estate.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.010
Of course, Tarkin wasn't the only CG character
in Rogue One.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:35.530
To create the droid K-2SO, the filmmakers
fitted Alan Tudyk with a motion capture suit

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:38.720
and then digitally added the robot's body
later.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.510
To get Tudyk's face at the appropriate level
for the towering robot, they took two different

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:44.510
approaches.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.990
In some scenes, Tudyk wore stilts while he
interacted with the other actors.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:53.840
If the shot was too dangerous to use stilts,
he wore backpack with a paper cutout of K-2's

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.830
head positioned several feet above his own.

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:58.020
Now that's dedication.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:02.210
"There's no way to respect this, you know?"

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:03.210
The Last Jedi

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:04.210
J.J.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:08.410
Abrams spoke at length about using as many
practical effects as possible in The Force

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:13.750
Awakens in order to re-create the feeling
of the original trilogy, and his successor

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:17.950
Rian Johnson adopted the same attitude when
he took the reins for The Last Jedi.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:21.830
Neal Scanlan returned to head up the creature
shop, and this time, they were tasked with

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:28.440
creating a new breed of animal called a vulptex,
a fox-like inhabitant of the planet Crait.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:29.810
Scanlan told Entertainment Weekly:

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:35.050
"The idea is that these wonderful sort of
feral creatures had lived on this planet and

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:39.000
had consumed the planet's surface, and as
such had become crystalline."

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:44.260
To get an idea of how such a creature might
move, they fitted a small dog with a suit

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:46.580
adorned with drinking straws.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:50.740
The next step was creating an animatronic
puppet that the cast could interact with in

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:56.400
scenes, but much like the BB-8 build, it wasn't
possible to use it 100 percent of the time.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.950
Static models complete with 25,000 individual
crystals were built for the benefit of the

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:07.020
VFX team, who were able to scan them and render
the creatures however Johnson wanted.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:08.450
Thanks for watching!

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.130
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:13.290
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

